TRAC MPIAE Changes
===================


Version 0.25.0 -- New mobile-first frontend variant
----------------------------------------------------
- NEW: add new frontend under frontend_new/, as an alternative to the old part of
  the frontend that was used to add activities.
  The new one is mobile first and component-based, without global state. It also
  computes participant state in the backend via a state machine instead of
  relying on redirects in the frontend. It is limited to one timeline, but
  supports several parallel activities in that timeline now. Basically it consists
  of 3 components: activity list, timeline, form to specify/edit activity details.
  The mobile version switches between them, the desktop version always shows the
  timeline and only switches, in a 2nd column, between activity selector and form.
  The timeline has all days under one another, in one long list, similar to a
  calendar. The desktop version also permanently shows a synced day reel on the
  left in a small third column to make navigation easier.
  This solves various problems of the old frontend, including the need for many
  buttons to switch the days and timelines, people not noticing the 2nd timeline,
  usability issues on mobile, and more.
  The current version also offers the old frontend, still functional. But study
  configuration should be adapted to the frontend/number of timelines.
  The new frontend saves activities (and can save one for several days at once),
  while the old one saved snapshots of entire days. This means they currently use
  different saving mechanisms and backend endpoints for loading and saving.
  Great care must be taken for these not to drift apart, and in the long term,
  a decision for one of the frontends is needed. This is an interim version that
  has both, but that won't be the state for long.



Version 0.24.0 -- Mobile improvements, backup endpoints, many fixes
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- NEW: on phones the timeline switcher is a pair of tabs naming both timelines,
  instead of a button labelled with the one you are already in. Testers reported
  they had "missed the existence of the 2nd timeline entirely", and the previous
  mitigation could not fix that: the picker only hinted at the second timeline
  with a "(1/2)" inside its own label, and a button labelled with the state you
  are currently in reads as a status readout - there is no reason to press it. A
  segmented control shows the track you are *not* on, so the second timeline
  exists without any interaction, and switching is one tap instead of opening a
  sheet and picking a row. Mobile only, and only for a study with exactly two
  timelines: one timeline has nothing to switch to (the control hides, as
  before) and three or more segments would run off the row, so those keep the
  picker. That rule is `shouldShowTimelineTabs()` in `constants.js`, pinned by
  `tests/unit/timeline_tabs.test.js` - no study has anything but two timelines,
  and a stubbed activities config with a different timeline set does not bring
  the diary up at all, so the other branches cannot be reached over E2E. The strip
  takes its own row under the day control, because two
  study-authored names do not fit beside it (measured: ~190 px day control plus
  ~300 px of segments in a 382 px row); German is the long case, and a guard
  asserts both names render in full rather than ellipsised, next to the existing
  no-sideways-scrolling sweep. Semantics are `role="tablist"`/`role="tab"` with
  `aria-selected`, a roving tabindex and Left/Right arrows; the picker keeps its
  caret (and its full label while the tabs are off) as the way into the timeline
  sheet, which still carries the per-timeline coverage and "Clear timeline".
  The segments drop the "(1/2)" counter, which stays in the canvas title.
  Guarded by `mobile_context_bar.spec.js`.
- CHG: the blue instruction banner now stays until the participant closes it, on
  every day of the study. It used to delete itself on any day but the first
  ("it only shows on first day of study, which is irritating. we should show it
  on all days, UNTIL user first closes it"), which inverted the intent: the gate
  was checked while the page initialised, and the day it looks at is the one the
  page was opened on (`day_label_index`, written on every day switch) - so it
  fired for the participant who came back on day 3 and had to remember the
  gestures, and never for the one sitting on day 1. Closing it is still
  remembered per participant and study, and the storage key string is unchanged
  (it keeps its historical `:day1` suffix on purpose - renaming it would show the
  banner again to everyone who had already dismissed it). The two i18n keys lose
  their misleading `dayOne` prefix: `banner.instructionsMobile` /
  `banner.instructionsDesktop` in all 7 locales, `index.html` and the
  required-keys list in `locales_consistency.test.js` (the text never mentioned a
  day). Guarded by `mobile_layout.spec.js > the instruction banner stays on every
  day until it is closed`: it loads straight into the second day - the case the
  old gate broke, with the day asserted so the test cannot pass while landing on
  day 1 - switches days, closes the banner, and checks it stays gone on other
  days and after a reload.
- NEW: activity blocks show the resize grips on phones too. Desktop has had them
  since the "like an Excel cell" feedback, but below the breakpoint they were
  hidden: the vertical layout needs the marker rotated onto the ends that can
  actually be dragged, the block's top and bottom edge. The same two spans are
  re-used (no markup change), so the bars simply become horizontal and sit flush
  in the band that `touch-action: none` reserves for the resize. Unlike desktop
  there is no hover on a touch device - a grip waiting for it would never appear
  and the gesture would stay undiscovered - so they are visible at a modest
  opacity and step up while the block is focused or being resized. Guarded by
  `mobile_block_scroll.spec.js`, which asserts both grips are rendered, are thin
  horizontal bars, never take the touch (`pointer-events: none`), and each sits
  inside its own resize band - a marker that is not where the gesture works is
  worse than no marker.
- FIX: footer links no longer take the participant out of the study. The default
  study's Contact link carried `in_new_tab: false`, so following it navigated the
  diary page away and the back button was the only way home ("if people click the
  footer links, they effectively left study"). Both flags are gone - the
  per-link `in_new_tab` and the deployment's `OPEN_LEGAL_LINKS_IN_NEW_TAB` - and
  the footer decides by origin: a `target_url` on our own server opens in a
  dialog over the page the participant is on (nothing navigates at all),
  anything external opens in a new tab with `rel="noopener noreferrer"` and says
  so in its accessible name plus a decoration marker. The dialog lives in
  `footer.js` because those pages load neither `ui.js` nor `styles.css`: it is a
  real dialog (role/aria-modal, labelled, Escape, backdrop, focus back on the
  link, scroll lock). External targets deliberately keep the new tab - another
  site can refuse framing (`X-Frame-Options`, `frame-ancestors`) and a blank
  frame cannot be detected from JS. The config parser now rejects the removed
  field (`extra="forbid"`), so a stale key fails loudly instead of being ignored.
  Guarded by `study_footer_links.spec.js` (external target/rel/accessible name,
  and the dialog: opens, keeps the URL, closes on Escape, restores focus).
- CHG: the footer no longer reports the backend connection. "Connected to
  backend" sat under the app name and version on every participant page, cost a
  line of space and made participants wonder what it referred to - it was
  diagnostics for us, not information for them. Removed together with its three
  locale keys (`footer.backend_status_*`) and the colour table that existed only
  to keep the line legible. Nothing is lost on a real failure: the diary still
  renders the fatal-error panel in the timeline area
  (`renderFatalInitializationError()`) and the "no studies available" / "study
  not found" messages; the status line was a duplicate of those and was hidden
  on the diary page anyway.
- NEW: on a phone an activity can be moved along the day again. There was no way
  to shift a whole block: interact.js' draggable is desktop-only, the long press
  owns the menu gesture, and the arrow keys move one edge each (they resize).
  The long-press menu now offers "Move earlier" / "Move later": 10-minute steps
  (the grid the desktop drag snaps to), duration preserved, stopping at the day's
  ends (04:00 / 04:00(+1)), and a move onto another activity flashes the block
  instead of happening - exactly how the arrow keys behave. Desktop keeps its
  drag, so its menu is unchanged. The tail those edits share (datasets, position,
  time chip, timeline state, button states) now lives in one helper used by the
  arrow keys and by the move. Guarded by `mobile_activity_gestures.spec.js`
  (step, duration, timeline state, the day's start as a boundary) and by the
  desktop menu staying move-free in `activity_context_menu_desktop.spec.js`.
- CHG: on a phone an activity block no longer runs into the right edge of the
  screen. Measured at 390 px it spanned 25 %-100 % of the timeline, flush against
  the border, and its time chip - positioned 50 px *outside* the block - was
  clipped to a ~1 px black sliver at the screen edge, which is what made a block
  look cut off. The block now spans 25 %-95 % (`MOBILE_BLOCK_WIDTH` 75 -> 70 in
  `script.js`) and the chip sits inside the block's right end (mobile
  `.time-label` `right: -50px` -> `4px`). Desktop is untouched: time is the
  horizontal axis there, so a block's width is its duration. Guarded by
  `mobile_layout.spec.js > an activity block and its time chip keep clear of the
  screen edge` (gap share, chip inside the block, chip on screen).
- CHG: the diary no longer announces that it is saving. The autosave status chip
  used to say "Saving..." within a second of every edit and "Saved" a moment
  later, which participants reported as the app being laggy - the write itself is
  debounced by ~2 s, so the chip drew attention to a wait they cannot act on. The
  chip now appears for exactly one reason: the day could NOT be stored, with its
  Retry that skips the engine's backoff. The saving states are silent; the
  `messages.syncSaving` / `messages.syncSaved` labels are removed from all 7
  locales, and the chip is built together with the toolbar so the failure has an
  `aria-live` region to be announced in (`sync_status.test.js`,
  `autosave.spec.js > the toolbar stays quiet while the day is being stored`).
- FIX: the backend now ships `psycopg` (v3) as its PostgreSQL driver instead of
  `psycopg2-binary`, and declares an explicit `sqlalchemy>=2.1` floor. SQLAlchemy
  2.1 changed the driver it loads for a plain `postgresql://` URL from psycopg2 to
  psycopg, so a fresh install of the wheel (as the wheel smoke test does, resolving
  dependencies unpinned) picked up SQLAlchemy 2.1.4 and then died on
  `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'psycopg'`. SQLModel had to move to 0.0.48
  (its versions below that cap SQLAlchemy to < 2.1) and uv.lock was re-resolved for
  sqlalchemy 2.1.4, sqlmodel 0.0.48, psycopg 3.3.6. No configuration changes are
  needed: every `postgresql://` URL (env templates, CI, Docker stacks) keeps working.
- NEW: make page title of instructions text configurable by user via study_text_instructions_title key in studies_config.json section of study.
- FIX: record the stored (DBMS-normalized) capture instant as the browser
  identification blob's `first_captured_at` instead of the pre-write Python
  timestamp. MySQL/MariaDB and SQL Server keep less sub-second precision than
  PostgreSQL, so the datetime column and the blob used to disagree on the same
  capture, which made the backend integration tests fail on those DBMS.
- FIX: a submitted study can no longer be edited through the API. The activity
  submission and consent endpoints now return HTTP 403 once the participant's
  `study_submitted_at` is set; previously only the frontend redirected submitted
  participants to the thank-you page, so the endpoints still accepted diary and
  consent changes when called directly. External task confirmations intentionally
  remain possible after submission.
- FIX: the consent page no longer reports permanent failures as temporary ones. A
  broken or expired participant link (400/403 `study_access_denied`), a paused or
  closed study (403 `study_unavailable`) and an already submitted study (403 on
  the consent POST) each get their own message now; before, every one of them
  ended in "please try again" / "reload the page", which could never succeed.
- FIX: the consent page retries transient failures (network error, 5xx) twice with
  linear backoff, like the diary's config sync, so a short backend restart during
  maintenance no longer shows an error at all. When the consent information cannot
  be loaded the accept/decline buttons now stay disabled - the page used to offer
  them anyway, so a participant could consent to text that never appeared (the
  "could not load / could not save" report from the MPIAE pilot study).
- DEV: the retry policy now lives in `frontend/src/js/api_request.js` and is
  shared by the diary config sync and the consent page; both the retry policy and
  the failure classification have unit tests, and the consent error states have
  Playwright coverage.
- NEW: activity blocks on desktop show a small grip on their left and right edge
  (hover, keyboard focus, or while resizing). The edges could always be dragged to
  adjust a duration, but nothing on the block said so, and participants asked for
  a visible marker in the style of an Excel fill handle. The grips are decoration
  (`pointer-events: none`), so the resize zones and cursors are unchanged; they
  are not shown on the phone layout (deferred, see `dev_tools/todo_mobile.md`).
- FIX: on a phone a swipe that starts on an activity block scrolls the day again.
  `touch-action: none` on `.activity-block` turned the block surface - about 75 %
  of the timeline width - into a scroll dead zone, reported as "the scrolling
  doesn't work on my phone"; measured with real touch input it moved the timeline
  0 px, while the same swipe 30 px further left scrolled it. The block body may
  pan vertically now, and the top/bottom bands keep `touch-action: none`
  (`activity-block-edge-zone`) so a resize is not cancelled by a scroll mid-drag.
- FIX: on a phone the day can be scrolled back up again. A document-level
  `touchmove` guard for pull-to-refresh called `preventDefault()` whenever
  `window.pageYOffset === 0` and the finger moved down - on the diary the window
  never scrolls (the day scrolls inside `.timelines-wrapper`), so the condition
  was permanently true and every downward drag was cancelled: the day could be
  swiped forward to its end, but the way back did nothing at all ("I cannot
  scroll up again, I'm stuck"). The guard is gone; pull-to-refresh stays off
  through `overscroll-behavior: none` alone, now set on the root element and the
  body. A mouse never triggers the old handler and the mobile suite drives the
  phone layout with the mouse, which is why it went unnoticed -
  `mobile_day_scroll.spec.js` covers the gesture with real touch input (CDP).
- FIX: on a phone the end of the day - its last hour line - can be scrolled into
  view. The content ended exactly on the bottom edge of the scroller, so at
  maximum scroll the line sat on the boundary and the day appeared to stop ~5
  minutes short of it. `.last-initialized-timeline-wrapper` now keeps 20 px of
  padding below the timeline (content-box, so the hour spacing is unchanged).
  Second half, reported from a Pixel after that change: the day *still* ended
  flush, 0 px of whitespace - the padding was there but invisible. The scroller
  was 20 px taller than the space it occupied: `.timeline-canvas` carried
  `margin: 20px 0` on top of a height already computed as `100vh` minus header
  and footer, so the scroller's bottom edge sat below the screen (measured at
  412x915: 935 px in a 915 px viewport) and the 20 px tail was exactly the part
  hanging off. The canvas now has no vertical margin (the breathing room under
  the header is padding inside it), the scroller fills it (`height: 100%`), and
  the mobile height uses `100svh` with a `100vh` fallback: `vh` is the viewport
  with the browser chrome *hidden*, and since the page itself never scrolls the
  URL bar stays - so the day's end could hide behind it. Result at 412x915: the
  day's last line now sits 20 px above the bottom of the screen.
- FIX: on a phone the block's resize ends are real again. `touch-action: none`
  on `.activity-block-edge-zone` was meant to reserve the top/bottom band of a
  block for resizing, but the base `.activity-block-edge-zone { display: none }`
  was declared *after* the mobile override with the same specificity, so the
  bands were never rendered (measured: `display: none`, height 0 - and a hidden
  element still reports `touch-action: none`, so the contract test passed while
  the reservation did not exist). The base rule now comes first, and the band is
  capped at `min(16px, 30%)` of the block so a 10-minute block (~17 px tall)
  keeps a scrollable middle - at 40 % the two bands left 3 px of it, which is the
  "scrolling doesn't work on my phone" dead zone again. The band test in
  `mobile_block_scroll.spec.js` now asserts a rendered height and that the resize
  drag starts on a band, so a silently hidden band cannot pass again.
- FIX: a timeline with no coverage requirement is no longer reported as
  "requirement met" while it is empty. `min_coverage: 0` is satisfied by nothing
  at all, so the secondary timeline of the pilot study showed a green ✓ next to
  its name before the participant had entered anything - read as "this one is
  done, skip it". A ✓ is now only shown where the study requires something and
  that requirement is met; no requirement plus no entries reads as "nothing
  entered yet" in a neutral colour, and the red shortfall text is unchanged. The
  timeline menu shows the same wording as the timeline itself (it had the same
  vacuous ✓), both from one formatter in `frontend/src/js/ui.js`.
- NEW: on a phone an untouched second timeline is advertised inside the day it
  belongs to. Below the breakpoint only the active timeline is laid out (every
  other container measures 0×0), so nothing on the page mentioned the other
  timeline and a participant who never opened the context bar's timeline picker
  never learned it existed. The active timeline's label block now lists one
  tappable row per other timeline that is still empty on the current day
  ("Secondary Activity · nothing entered yet ›"); a tap switches timelines
  through the same path the picker uses, and the row disappears as soon as that
  timeline has an entry. Desktop shows both timelines side by side and gets no
  rows.
- NEW: every place a timeline names itself now also says where it sits in the
  set - "Main Activity (1/2)": the timeline title, the phone context bar, the
  timeline menu and the desktop switch-timeline button. Same compact pattern the
  phone day counter already uses ("(3/7)"), and plain numbers, so it needed no
  translation. A study with a single timeline is unchanged ("(1/1)" would
  announce a choice that does not exist).
- NEW: the instructions page no longer pushes the study's own text behind a
  reading assignment. The generic walk-through ("Visual Illustrations", 4
  animated GIFs plus helpful tools) is a closed section now and the Start button
  is pinned on the phone layout: measured at 390x664 the page went from 4779 px
  (7.2 screens, Start at px 4623 as the last element of the document) to 1524 px
  (2.3 screens, Start inside the first screen). Nothing is removed - one tap
  opens the steps, and the study's own intro/instruction text, which is the
  study's protocol rather than ours, keeps its place at the top. The bottom of a
  desktop page is a hairline action row now instead of a 101 px white slab with
  an upward shadow wedged between the text and the footer links (~70 px of dead
  space above it, the button hugging the far right of the column), and the
  redundant "please click the blue Start button" line is gone: the button says
  Start and it is right there.
- FIX: the instructions page no longer stretches on a window taller than itself.
  The body is a grid, and its auto rows stretched to fill the viewport, so the
  Start row grew (163 px at 1920x1200, 196 px at 1600x1300, plus a 140 px footer
  row) and the button - a flex item with the default `align-items: stretch` -
  grew with it into a square (140x131). Rows are content-sized now and the row
  centers its button, so the page simply ends where its content ends.
- DEV: the local dev server now supports phone testing on the same wifi without
  storing any address in the repo. The dev frontend settings call the API
  same-origin (`/tud_backend/api`, like production), and `run_dev_nginx_both.bash`
  derives this machine's LAN address on every start, adds it to
  `TUD_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` and points `TUD_FRONTEND_URL` at it in the generated
  `backend/.env` (the committed template stays localhost-only, so another machine
  or the same one on another network needs no edits; `TUD_DEV_LAN_IP=…` overrides
  the detection). The dev nginx `/favicon.ico` alias was also fixed - it pointed
  outside `src/`, so every page load logged a 404 and showed no tab icon.
- NEW: "How to use the diary" in the ⋮ menu (both layouts) opens the walk-
  through in a modal instead of leaving the day. It is the instructions page in
  `?embed=1` mode - same steps, GIFs and translations, without the study text,
  the language pill or the Start button - loaded in an iframe so there is no
  second copy of four animated GIFs to maintain. Leaving the page would be safe
  (the day autosaves and comes back from the URL) but costs a page load, the
  scroll position and the selected activity, and mid-task error pages are what
  the pilot's "could not load" report was about.
- FIX: a submitted study can no longer be overwritten through the copy-day
  endpoint. `POST /api/studies/{study}/participants/{p}/day_labels/{target}/copy-from/{source}`
  deletes the target day's rows and rewrites them, but it never checked
  `study_submitted_at`, so the one write path that replaces a whole day was also
  the one path that bypassed the immutability promise the other endpoints
  enforce (day save and consent both return 403). That matters beyond the diary:
  a researcher may already have exported a participant's submitted data, and a
  silent overwrite afterwards would make the export disagree with the server
  without any error. It now returns 403 for a submitted participant, in both
  copy directions; `test_post_submission_lock.py` covers the pre-submission copy
  (still allowed, it is how a second day is filled in) plus the refusal.
- NEW: three read-only, super-admin-only endpoints for extracting data into a
  separate backup system: `GET /api/admin/participants` (one row per
  participant-per-study, with `submitted`/`study_submitted_at` and per-day
  coverage status), `GET /api/admin/activities` (one row per reported activity)
  and `GET /api/admin/studies` (study metadata, day labels, timelines and
  activity definitions). The existing `/api/admin/export/{study}/...` pair is
  built for scientists doing statistical analysis and repeats participant
  columns on every activity row, which makes it unusable as a relational copy.
  The intended loop is: poll `participants?submitted=true&submitted_after=<ts>`
  for who finished, then pull exactly those participants' rows from
  `activities?participant=...`. A participant counts as done when
  `study_submitted_at` is set, which the backend only allows once every day met
  its timeline `min_coverage`; external tasks are deliberately ignored. Because a
  submitted diary is immutable (see the fix above), each participant only has to
  be pulled once. `study` (repeatable) narrows both extracts, `format=csv|json`
  picks the rendering, and an empty result is a `200` with zero records rather
  than the 404 the scientific exports raise - "nobody has finished yet" must not
  look like a failed request to the backup script.
- NEW: read-only backup accounts (`TUD_API_BACKUP_USERS`), so the export job no
  longer has to run with the super-admin credential. An account listed there can
  call the three extraction endpoints and nothing else; every other admin route
  answers 403. The restriction is enforced in `require_admin_identity`, the one
  gate all admin routes already share, because study scope alone would not cover
  the routes without a study name in their path - the admin overview, the
  file-validation tools and "create study from files" would otherwise have
  accepted the account. Entries are validated like the scientist ones, with two
  deliberate differences: a username must not appear in `TUD_API_ADMIN_USERNAME`
  or `TUD_API_SCIENTISTS` (the backend refuses to start, since the effective role
  would otherwise depend on lookup order), and unsupported fields are rejected
  rather than ignored - a `studies` key would have suggested a restriction that
  does not exist, because a backup account is server-wide by design.
  `AdminIdentity.can_access_study()` also returns False for the role, so a stale
  owner entry naming a backup account can never grant it study access. Coverage:
  `tests/unit/test_settings_backup_users.py`, the backup cases in
  `tests/unit/test_admin_identity.py`, and
  `tests/integration/test_admin_extract_endpoints.py` (allowed on exactly the
  three extracts, 403 `extraction_only_account` on a representative set of the
  others).
- NEW: the activity extract can be paged, and the participant extract no longer
  costs the whole study. A full-server copy used to build the entire response in
  memory: measured at ~670 bytes per activity row in JSON and ~3.9 KB of RAM per
  row, one thousand participants with seven days and thirty entries each (210k
  rows) was already a 141 MB response and +785 MB of resident memory, so a
  "few thousand participants" study would have needed several GB and a couple of
  minutes for a single request. `GET /api/admin/activities` now returns one page
  of participants at a time (`limit`, default 200, max 2000; `cursor` from the
  previous page's `next_cursor`), and reports the state in both the JSON
  metadata envelope and response headers (`X-Next-Cursor`, `X-Has-More`,
  `X-Participants-Returned`, `X-Record-Count`) so a CSV consumer can follow it
  too. Paging is by **participant**, not by row offset: saving a day deletes and
  re-inserts that day's rows, so row positions shift under a concurrent write
  while participant keys do not; a page is a half-open `(study_id,
  participant_id)` key range, which also keeps one participant out of two pages.
  Passing an explicit `participant` selection still returns everything in one
  unpaged response (that is the documented workflow: poll `/participants` for
  who is done, then pull those participants). Separately,
  `_build_participant_completion_map()` gained an optional participant filter and
  `/api/admin/participants` now passes the participants it is about to return:
  the helper loads every activity row of the study to derive per-day coverage,
  so without that the endpoint took 2.8 s and +371 MB for a 0.33 MB payload, and
  the `submitted` filter made no difference to the cost because the map was built
  before it was applied. Coverage: the paging cases in
  `tests/unit/test_admin_extract.py` (every participant exactly once, a page
  never splits a participant, paging across a study boundary whose participant
  ids sort before the first study's, invalid/empty cursors, cursor+participant
  rejected) and a two-page-over-HTTP walk in
  `tests/integration/test_admin_extract_endpoints.py`.
- NEW: the scientist activities export (`GET /api/admin/export/{study}/activities`)
  can now be analysed on a continuous time axis. Each row gains the derived
  columns `start_minutes_since_diary_start` and `end_minutes_since_diary_start`
  (minutes since the start of the first reporting day, so day 0 22:00 is 1320 and
  04:00 on day 1 is 1440) plus `day_label_index`, and `study_day_start_minutes`
  (metadata columns) documents the origin. These are computed on read from the
  stored clock values - nothing new is written to the database, and
  `start_minutes`/`end_minutes` stay the authoritative pair. The values are
  anchored to the first reporting day, not to midnight, because the day window of
  every current study starts at 04:00. The same endpoint now also orders rows by
  `DayLabel.display_order`; it previously used the internal `day_label_id`, so a
  study whose label ids were not in display order produced a non-chronological
  file. Pinned by `tests/unit/test_coverage.py` (both day windows) and
  `test_api_endpoint_structure.py::test_admin_activities_export_has_minutes_since_diary_start`
  (values, ordering, CSV header, metadata on/off).


Version 0.23.0 -- Minor text adjustments
----------------------------------------
- NEW: make instructions page title user configurable
- CHG: change default german welcome text


Version 0.22.0 -- Optional opt-in browser identification
--------------------------------------------------------
- NEW: browser/device identification, so researchers can tell whether the app
  works for participants on different devices (it complements the questions
  participants are asked about their experience). A study opts in with the new
  `save_browser_identification` setting. For this, `ua-parser-js` is vendored
  in `frontend/src/assets/ua-parser-js/` at version 1.0.41, the last
  MIT-licensed release.
- DEV: migration 0011 adds `studies.save_browser_identification` and
  `study_participants.user_agent` / `client_info` /
  `client_info_captured_at`.
- DEV: `README_CREATE_STUDY.md` documents the setting as opt-in and points out
  that the data is personal and usage of the feature belongs in the consent text,
  and `README.md` documents the new participant export alongside the activities export.


Version 0.21.0 -- New mobile interface, i18n hardening, clarify DB migration policy
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- CHG: drop the duplicate "Save Day" button from the diary footer. Both it and
  the button in the toolbar above the timeline called the very same save
  routine, and the toolbar one is the one users reach (it is sticky on phones).
  The footer now collapses entirely while it has no visible action, which is the
  case for every study with `allow_skip_timeuse: false`: an empty action row was
  costing a full row of vertical space on phones.
- NEW: the database schema is documented as forward-only, and that policy now has
  tests. `backend/tests/migrations/` (run with the new `./test_backend_migrations.sh`,
  which starts a throwaway database) executes the real migrations, which no other
  suite did - the integration tests build their schema with
  `SQLModel.metadata.create_all()` and so never run them:
  * `upgrade head` on an empty database, and upgrading twice,
  * upgrading a database that already holds data from *every* revision to head and
    then running `tud studies import` against it (the deployment that actually
    happens, and the only place 0004's data conversion runs),
  * adopting a database created by `create_all()` before Alembic existed via
    `stamp` + `upgrade`,
  * `alembic check`: a change in `models.py` without a migration now fails CI.
  There is deliberately no downgrade test: reverting a migration that dropped a
  column cannot restore the data, and 0004 cannot reverse its type change at all.
- FIX: adopting a database that was created before Alembic existed failed on
  MariaDB. Migration 0004 changed `studies.description` to JSON before rewriting
  its values, but MariaDB validates the rows that are already in the table when
  the column becomes JSON, so a legacy plain-text description aborted the whole
  upgrade (`CONSTRAINT studies.description failed`). The values are now made
  JSON-safe first - `JSON_VALID()` keeps objects and arrays, `JSON_QUOTE()` turns
  plain text into a JSON string - and the column type is changed afterwards.
  Found by the new migration tests, which now also run on MariaDB.
- NEW: `.github/workflows/migrations.yml` runs those tests on every push and pull
  request (PostgreSQL), and on MariaDB/MSSQL on demand
  (`gh workflow run migrations.yml -f run_dbms_matrix=true`) - their migration
  branches (0004's type conversion, 0010's constraint/index handling) had never
  been executed on those DBMSes.
- DEV: `README.md` ("Database schema policy"), `docs/agents/database.md` and the
  database skill describe the forward-only policy: migrations stay additive for the
  deployed code (nullable/server defaults, data moves in its own step, columns are
  dropped a release after they stop being used), undoing a release means redeploying
  the previous release or restoring a backup, and a pre-Alembic database is adopted
  once with `alembic stamp`.
- DEV: `backend/alembic.ini` declares `path_separator = os` (Alembic >= 1.11), which
  removes the deprecation warning about splitting `prepend_sys_path`.
- FIX (CI, surfaced by the first run with the new guards): the WebKit leg
failed because the activity info dialog carried `role="dialog"` on its inner
wrapper while only the overlay around it is shown and hidden. `js/ui.js`
detects open/close by watching the style of the `[role="dialog"]` element, so
that dialog counted as permanently open: focus was never given back, and WebKit
keeps focus on a control that just became invisible (Chromium moves it out by
itself, which is why only WebKit complained). The role/aria attributes now sit
on the overlay like every other dialog, the visibility check additionally
requires a layout box, and focus is blurred when the element that opened the
dialog cannot take it (the body is not focusable).
- FIX (CI): `frontend/tests/e2e/mobile_layout.spec.js` failed on WebKit because
the phone timeline is a tall column inside a clipped pane and the legal footer
is painted over the part that overflows it - tapping "the middle of the
timeline, clamped to the viewport" landed on a footer link, which no browser
routes to the timeline. Both specs now ask the new `findTimelinePoint()` helper
in `e2e_helpers.js` for a point the timeline actually receives.
- DEV: the e2e workflow no longer marks the `Europe/Berlin` leg as failed after
a green test run - the artifact name contained the matrix timezone and artifact
names may not contain a forward slash, so the upload step (not the tests) failed;
the test step now exports a slash-free `TZ_LABEL` and the uploads use it.
- DEV: `no_broken_static_requests.spec.js` ignores requests the browser itself
cancelled (Firefox and WebKit abort font downloads they stop needing; a missing
file answers 404, which the response handler still catches) and waits for
`document.fonts.ready` before asserting, so the guard is not timing-dependent.
- FIX (i18n text): the thank-you page's built-in message named the app, so it
read as "...Ihre Teilnahme an unserer Studie TRAC" - as if the participant had
signed up for TRAC instead of for the study. This is a leftover from when the app
was called "TimeUse" and the sentence was built around the app name; the app name
is now gone from `thankYou.defaultMessage` in every locale (en, de, es, fi, fr,
pl, sv) and from the static fallback in `frontend/src/pages/thank-you.html` (the
`.app-name` rule went with it). Study-specific end texts come from the study
config and are unaffected - they are simply shown instead of the default.
- FIX (accessibility, found by the new axe check): the diary's "Skip time
reporting" button rendered white text on its light grey background (1.23:1 - the
label was effectively invisible) because a later `.btn { color: white }` rule
overrode `.skip-btn`; the legal-footer links were #ccc on the page background
(1.53:1) and so were unreadable on the German pages; the timeline helper texts
and the inactive-timeline title were grey on grey (3.28:1 / 2.68:1); the idle
timer readout was just under the limit (4.09:1); the timeline containers carried
an `aria-label` without a role (assistive tech drops it); and the viewport meta
blocked pinch-zoom (`user-scalable=no`).
- FIX (phone layout): pages that do not load the shared stylesheet combined
`width: 100%` with `box-sizing: content-box`, so on a 390px viewport the open
studies page ran 77px wide, tasks 26px, timeout 22px and the German instructions
page 58px - `overflow-x: hidden` then cut the content off instead of showing it.
The diary timeline ran 4px wide (nested `width: 100%` + padding), and the German
legal-footer row could not wrap at all (inline links with no break opportunity),
so "Impressum - Datenschutz - Studieninformation" was clipped; the footer links
are also 24px tall now instead of 16px (WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8).
- DEV: new accessibility guard `frontend/tests/e2e/accessibility.spec.js` runs
axe-core (WCAG 2.0/2.1/2.2 A + AA) over every participant page plus the activity
picker and activity info dialogs, and fails on any violation; `@axe-core/playwright`
is a dev dependency. Verified to fail when the zoom-blocking meta is restored.
- DEV: new `frontend/tests/e2e/keyboard_accessibility.spec.js` covers the dialog
focus management in `js/ui.js` (focus moves into the opened dialog, Tab and
Shift+Tab cannot leave it, Escape closes it and returns focus) and the timeline
containers (named `role="group"`, focusable); `frontend/tests/e2e/mobile_layout.spec.js`
checks that no participant page scrolls sideways on a phone, that the main diary
controls are at least 24x24 and that an activity can be placed with touch events
alone. All three were verified to fail when the fixed behaviour is reverted.
- DEV: `frontend/tests/e2e/participant_pages.js` now holds the list of
participant pages, so the static-asset, accessibility and mobile guards sweep the
same set (this added the tasks page to the static-request check).
- FIX: the diary's overlap validation (used by the drag/resize/undo handlers, which
revert an invalid change) compared `new Date("07:30")` - an Invalid Date in every
engine - so it could never fire and invalid drags were kept. It now compares the
absolute minutes-of-day via `getActivityTimeRangeMinutes()`; `Timeline.validate()`
in `frontend/src/js/timeline.js` is covered by
`frontend/tests/unit/timeline_validation.test.js`.
- DEV: removed the unused `formatTimeDDMMYYYYHHMM()` helper, which derived real
calendar dates from the browser's local "today" (activities carry minutes-of-day,
not dates).
- DEV: the E2E workflow runs a second chromium leg with `TZ=Europe/Berlin` so
timezone/DST assumptions surface in CI instead of on a participant's phone.
- FIX: the diary's unsaved work is now kept via `frontend/src/js/draft_storage.js`,
which never throws when storage is unavailable or full (Safari private mode,
"block all cookies", quota exceeded - one `localStorage.setItem` on the
instruction banner was unguarded) and discards stored drafts that are corrupt,
structurally unusable or written by an older app version instead of feeding them
to the timeline. The participant/study/day check now lives in
`matchesTimelineContext()`, covered by unit tests.
- DEV: new `frontend/tests/unit/draft_storage.test.js` (quota, blocked storage,
corrupt JSON, unusable payloads, freshness window, other-participant draft,
clearing) and E2E `frontend/tests/e2e/draft_restore.spec.js` asserting that
unsaved activities survive a reload (verified to fail when the restore is
disabled).
- DEV: new static-asset guards: `frontend/tests/unit/static_assets.test.js`
checks that every asset referenced by HTML/CSS exists, and the new E2E spec
`frontend/tests/e2e/no_broken_static_requests.spec.js` visits all participant
pages and fails on any static request that does not succeed (missing GIF,
locale, settings file, font) - the class of bug that only the deployment used to
notice.
- DEV: `frontend/tests/unit/locale_values.test.js` guards translation *values*:
`{{placeholders}}` and the HTML tags of every key must match the reference
locale, so a translator cannot drop `{{days}}` or break `data-i18n-html` markup
without the suite complaining.
- FIX: the admin "study not found" page reflected the study name from the URL
path unescaped (`/admin/study/<img src=x onerror=...>` executed script in the
administrator's authenticated browser). Values are escaped now; new guards:
`tests/unit/test_html_escaping_guards.py` (no f-string HTML in HTMLResponse, no
`|safe` in admin templates) and `tests/integration/test_admin_html_escaping.py`
(reflected URL value + stored study name are rendered escaped).
- FIX: half-translated pages (e.g. German "Tagebuch absenden" heading above an
English body on the instructions page) were caused by a stale browser-cached
locale file: i18n.t() keeps the built-in English markup text for keys it cannot
resolve, so an old de.json mixes languages once a page uses new keys.
- FIX: js/i18n.js now revalidates the locale file (fetch cache: 'no-cache') and,
if keys were still missing, re-fetches it once with a cache-buster and applies
the fresh copy before reporting the remaining keys as a real translation gap.
- NEW: I18n.tOptional()/has() for optional keys (deployment-overridable labels
like footer.imprint) so expected misses no longer log warnings or trigger a
locale re-fetch; footer.js uses them.
- NEW: pages/timeout.html initialised no i18n at all (title/heading/message were
English for everyone) and the inactivity redirect passed no language; both are
fixed (timeout.title/heading/message, language appended to the redirect URL).
- FIX: missing i18n wiring on other pages: consent language label + select
aria-label, consent tooltips/review prompt/load error, consent page title per
study, thank-you default outro text, "Access study '<name>'" and the error text
on the open-studies page, tasks page title, the Submit Study tooltip and the
document title on the diary page.
- FIX: the instructions page "Loading..." GIF placeholder was hardcoded in
instructions.css; it is a real translated element now (common.loading). The GIF
alt texts are translated too (new data-i18n-alt support in i18n.js).
- FIX: messages.copyFromDay was referenced by script.js/ui.js but missing from
all 7 locale files, so the "Copy from..." button showed the raw key.
- DEV: new frontend/tests/unit/page_i18n_keys.test.js asserts that every key
requested by markup/scripts exists in every locale and that no stylesheet
contains hardcoded user-visible text; new i18n_missing_keys.test.js covers the
revalidation/repair/optional-lookup behaviour.


Version 0.20.0 -- New user type Scientist
------------------------------------------
- NEW: scoped admin accounts ("scientists") via TUD_API_SCIENTISTS. A scientist fully manages the studies she owns or was granted; super admins (TUD_API_ADMIN_USERNAME) keep unrestricted access.
- NEW: studies can have several owners, shown on the admin overview and managed as chips in the "Study Owners" section of the study detail page (editable after "Enable Editing").
- NEW: per-owner endpoints POST/DELETE /api/admin/studies/{study_name_short}/owners/{username} (idempotent, one audit entry each); PATCH stays as the batch/unowned path.
- NEW: a study created by a scientist is owned by that scientist; super-admin-created and CLI/import-config studies stay unowned.
- CHG: POST /api/admin/studies/import-config and the all-studies runtime config export are super-admin-only; single-study export stays available to its owners.
- DEV: migration 0009 adds studies.owner_usernames; all admin authentication and study-scope checks live in api_deps/admin_auth.py (see README, "Study ownership and scientist accounts").
- DEV: scientist integration tests also cover the positive paths (create study, manage participants, delete own study, env-granted study, single-study export has no other study's data).
- DEV: new Playwright spec frontend/tests/e2e/admin_study_owners.spec.js covers the owners UI.
- FIX: resetting a single participant's data (DELETE .../participants/{participant_id}/data) now also clears the study submission, so the participant can record again.
- DEV: fix brittle E2E tests that made the CI red (copy-day picker click-outside, skip-reporting button race, reset test).
- FIX: instructions page section 3 described the old "Submit" flow; it now explains Save Day / Submit Study with the real button labels.
- CHG: remove the stale buttons.submit, buttons.next and buttons.finishStudy labels from all locales; the save buttons now use buttons.saveDay.
- DEV: remove dead pre-Copy-Days code (#confirmationModal, #saveBtn, loading modal + CSS, utils.canFinishStudy(), unused imports), which also drops the utils.js -> ui.js import cycle.
- DEV: remove 32 unused i18n keys from all 7 locale files (verified against every literal key reference in frontend/src, frontend/tests and backend/src).
- DEV: document study_text_instructions and the text rendering/fallback rules in README_CREATE_STUDY.md and docs/agents/frontend.md.
- CHG: move the instructions-page default text from pages/instructions.js into the locales as instructions.instructionsDefault.
- FIX: study creation and rename conflicts now name the clashing study instead of a generic "already exists" message, and the file-validation page shows them readably.
- CHG: the long study `name` no longer has to be unique.
- NEW: instructions page gained step 3 "Copy a Day" (hidden for single-day studies); the Save/Submit step moved to step 4.
- DEV: copy-day texts live under instructions.step3.*; visibility is decided in applyCopyDayStepVisibility() from study_days_count.
- FIX: frontend type checking failed on CI because package.json depended on the placeholder package `tsc`, which shadowed the real compiler; that dependency is removed.


